  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Abraham @ Kuttiachan vs Food Inspector on 14 March, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Monday, The 14Th Day Of March 2016/24Th Phalguna, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 1258 of 2012 () -------------------------------- Crl.A 468/2002 Of The Additional Sessions Court (Adhoc)-Ii, Thalassery Cc 740/1998 of J.M.F.C., Kuthuparamba Revision Petitioner/Appellant/Ist Accused: --------------------------------------- Abraham @ Kuttiachan, Aged 44 Years S/O.Abraham, Moolechalil House, Thondiyil P.O. Peravoor, Kannur District. By Adv. Sri.Cibi Thomas Respondents/Complainant & State: --------------------------------- 1. Food Inspector Kuthuparamba Circle, Kuthuparamba-670 643. 2. State Of Kerala Rep. By Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala Ernakulam. R By Public Prosecutor Sri.R.Githesh This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 14-03-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: Scl. Abraham @ Kuttiachan vs Food Inspector on 14 March, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/173187486/ 1 B. Sudheendra Kumar, J. ------------------------------------- Crl.R.P.No. 1258 of 2012 -------------------------------------- Dated this the 14th day of March, 2016 Order",
    "facts": "The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 20.07.1998 at about 1.00 p.m., PW1, the Food Inspector purchased 750 gram of cowpea from the first accused. The first accused is the vendor and the second accused is the father of the first accused, who was the owner of the shop. After sampling as per rules, one sample was sent to the Public Analyst for analysis. The Public Analyst, after analysis submitted Ext.P18 report stating that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for the food item under the PFA Rules, 1955 and was therefore adulterated. Thereafter, on the request of the accused, the second sample was sent to the Central Food Laboratory for analysis. Ext.P26 certificate of Central Food Laboratory would also show that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for the food item as per the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules and was therefore adulterated. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW4 were examined and Exts.P1 to P27 were marked for the prosecution. DW1 was examined and Exts.D1 to D6 were marked for the revision petitioner. 5. The trial court convicted the revision petitioner for the violation of the licence as well as for selling the adulterated food article. The incident in this case was on 20.7.1998. The revision petitioner obtained the licence under the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules only on 7.9.1998. Therefore, the revision petitioner did not have a valid licence under the PFA Rules during the relevant period.",
